<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=marine-chronometer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marine chronometer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A marine chronometer is a precision timepiece carried on a ship to find its longitude by comparing Greenwich Mean Time with local time, first developed in the 18th century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> carpenter John Harrison's H4 of 1761 solved the precision problems of keeping time at sea. Harrison submitted it for the £20,000 longitude prize that the British government had offered in 1714. Pierre Le Roy's detent escapement and the simplified escapements of Thomas Earnshaw and John Arnold shaped later instruments, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was supplying routinely to its ships by 1825.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marine-chronometer.jpg" alt="Marine chronometer" width="250" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phantom Photographer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H1_low_250.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1761</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>John Harrison · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Christiaan Huygens built a balance-spring chronometer in 1675 that kept poor time at sea, and some see Pierre Le Roy’s of 1766 as the basis of the modern chronometer.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock">Mechanical clock<span>by 1300</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=marine-chronometer">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
